docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:18:Build a new grid-based solver under `src/solvers/` — a two-velocity-field, APIC-transfer, constraint-incompressible multiphase method — that handles all three target interactions in one method: water↔water (incompressible, with impact cavities), granular bed↔itself (Drucker-Prager elastoplasticity, angle of repose, tamping memory), and water↔bed with porosity (Darcy percolation at physical infiltration rates through a deformable, swelling bed, with pour craters). The work is structured as a validation ladder of rungs (L0 water → L1 bed → L2 percolation → L3 full coupling), each rung with quantitative exit gates. This is a pure bet on the novel architecture: a failed gate stops the ladder for redesign — there is no pre-committed fallback path.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:26:- **main (MPM + global Poisson):** plausible bed deformation, but (a) the pressure solve consumes ~70% of a ~30 ms frame and sparse RBGS (PR #18, 1.93×) is its practical ceiling — Jacobi-CG, adaptive iterations, and indirect dispatch all failed because the real bug is operator inconsistency (compact stride-1 solve vs central stride-2 divergence, A ≠ D·G → a divergence floor no solver beats); (b) water pools above the bed unreasonably long because Darcy resistance is formulated as a divergence penalty in the pressure RHS, which imposes a pressure-jump entry barrier at the bed surface — inconsistent with the correct Stokes–Darcy interface conditions (flux + stress continuity, not pressure continuity); (c) the bed is a fixed scaffold with a scalar porosity — no granular mechanics at all.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:27:- **rewrite (XPBD/PBF):** percolation, wetting/swelling (volume-conserving), and granular statics all work and are test-gated — but position projection derives velocity *after* the constraint solve, so impact momentum dies in the density projection. Both impact-coupling attempts (water→grain committed-dormant; water↔water reverted in `4dca52f`) confirmed there is no contact-pressure layer to patch. No craters, no cavities, no tamping memory.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:55:- R10. The solver is one complete entry behind the `Solver` trait (`src/solvers/base.rs`): six methods, canonical `ParticleBuffers` layout, `Metrics`/`Profile`, three-edit registration (`src/engine/solvers.json`, `SolverId`, `build_solver`). Existing scenes, `src/models/` physics laws, and the V60 geometry are reused, not duplicated. The XPBD solver and existing tests remain untouched and green.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:61:- KTD-1 — **Two velocity fields per grid node (water + solid), APIC particle↔grid transfers.** Percolation requires co-located phases at *different* velocities (relative Darcy flux); contact requires momentum exchange on a shared grid. One field per phase satisfies both; velocity-averaging couplers (Genesis) are structurally rejected. APIC (per-particle affine matrix C) is the momentum carrier that makes cavities — its absence is precisely why XPBD craters failed. Per the v1 data-path lesson (`docs/issues/010-separate-water-bed-data-paths.md`), water and solid particles occupy separate buffer ranges with phase-specific dispatches, not a shared branchy buffer.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:62:- KTD-2 — **Incompressibility = coarse-grid pressure seed + fine local Jacobi cleanup, on one discretely consistent operator family.** The coarse solve (e.g. 1/4³ resolution) kills the low-frequency modes local iteration can't reach (this is the unbuilt PB-MPM 3D-convergence fix — the novel core); a fixed budget of fine Jacobi sweeps kills high-frequency residual. No converged Poisson (main's graveyard), no EOS-only fallback for the shipped path (an adaptive-Tait weak-compressibility predictor, c_s ≈ 10·v_max, is permitted as a pre-projection stabilizer if implementation finds it helps). **Operator consistency is a stated gate, not an aspiration:** divergence, gradient, and both grids' Laplacians come from one MAC-consistent family, the coarse operators are consistent restrictions of the fine ones, and rung L0 must measure the post-projection divergence and show it decays below a pre-registered tolerance as the iteration budget grows — a floor that plateaus above tolerance is the failure, and it is the exact signature that killed main's adaptive-RBGS, so it must be demonstrably absent. Two-field corollary from production Euler-Euler practice (the multiphase Rhie-Chow lesson, Cubero 2007 / Ferreira 2021): whatever velocity feeds the divergence must carry the drag and solids-pressure contributions consistently with the momentum update, or φ_s develops spurious oscillations even when the pressure operators themselves are consistent.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:65:- KTD-5 — **Bed constitutive model: Klar et al. 2016 Drucker-Prager return map** (3-branch, log-strain, one 3×3 SVD per solid particle per substep) with saturation-dependent cohesion from `src/models/cohesion.rs` (Bishop-style effective stress) and a compaction cap surface for tamping memory (plastic volumetric strain persists and feeds packing fraction → permeability). The tensile-apex branch is what lets crater walls stand and then collapse. **Stress partition is stated once and audited:** total stress = effective stress (DP + cap, with the U5 over-packing solids-pressure guard living INSIDE the contact/effective partition) + pore pressure, and the projection pressure IS the pore pressure — no volumetric mode may be resisted twice by contact pressure, cap pressure, solids-pressure guard, and projection pressure stacking up. The U7 stress-partition audit gates this.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:66:- KTD-6 — **Cavity sustain via constraint bubbles** (Goldade & Batty 2017): free-surface cells are tracked; enclosed air pockets get one aggregate volume constraint each in the pressure solve, so the pour cavity doesn't instantly re-level. Rung L0 ships the free-surface treatment plus at minimum the single-bubble case (the pour cavity is one pocket); generalization to arbitrary pocket counts may defer.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:67:- KTD-7 — **GPU layout discipline:** P2G scatter uses fixed-point `atomic<i32>` accumulation (KEEP.md §3 pattern, FP_SCALE re-validated against the velocity cap with documented overflow headroom); bind groups are split by pass family (transfers / pressure / plasticity / coupling) per `docs/issues/003-split-mpm-bind-groups.md`; every entry point fits the device's storage-buffer request. **Discrepancy to resolve in U1:** `src/utils/gpu.rs` currently requests exactly 9 storage buffers (`NEEDED_STORAGE_BUFFERS`) while AGENTS.md sanctions 16 — the scaffold unit raises the request only if the two-field grid genuinely needs it and verifies browser grants; grid fields are packed into vec4 lanes first.
