docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:129:- **Approach:** Mirror the xpbd module shape: one Pod `Params` byte-mirrored in WGSL with a size assert; auto-layout pipelines via `make`/`bg` closures; stable global binding indices; all passes through a `dispatch_pass`-style helper feeding `Profile`. Separate water/solid particle ranges in the canonical `ParticleBuffers` layout so `ui::Renderer` works unchanged. Resolve the 9-vs-16 storage-buffer discrepancy here: pack grid fields into vec4 lanes first, raise `NEEDED_STORAGE_BUFFERS` only if required, and verify a web build still gets the device.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:130:- **Patterns to follow:** `src/solvers/xpbd/mod.rs` (Params, pipelines, dispatch_pass, freeze/apply comments); `src/engine/registry.rs` three-edit pattern and its in-module catalog test (auto-extends via `SolverId::all()`); `tests/phase0.rs` runtime solver-switch test.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:131:- **Test scenarios:** registry row exists for the new `SolverId` (extend the existing registry test's coverage); `build` + one `step` on an empty scene leaves finite state and returns cached metrics without stalling; `Profile.dispatches_per_frame` > 0 and recorded; `particles()` returns the canonical layout with a populated `phase_tag` buffer for a water-only scene (the existing `tests/render_smoke.rs` already validates rendering for any conforming `ParticleBuffers` — no twofield mirror needed).
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:140:- **Approach:** Quadratic B-spline weights (KEEP.md §3 validated math), fixed-point `atomic<i32>` P2G scatter with documented FP_SCALE/velocity-cap headroom, per-particle affine C matrix carried and updated in G2P. Gravity on the grid, plus grid-node solid-boundary treatment against the scene SDF solids (separating/no-penetration condition on grid velocities, mirroring `src/utils/sdf.rs` conventions with a "mirrors utils::sdf" WGSL comment) — boundary handling is scoped work here, not an afterthought. No pressure yet — this unit's water is a momentum-correct splashing dust.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:155:- **Test scenarios:** operator unit gates run BEFORE any fluid test (the production MMS/code-verification pattern, Salari-Knupp): discrete adjointness `u·(Gp) = −(Du)·p` to machine precision for random fields on the staggered layout, the same check for the coarse restriction/prolongation pair, a manufactured-solution order-of-accuracy sweep on D/G/L (observed log-log error slope matches the formal order), and — decisively — the same checks on the ASSEMBLED projection operator `A = −D·M⁻¹·G` exactly as used in the solve (mass/φ weighting, solid masks, boundary rows included), at both fine and coarse-with-transfer levels, on masked domains representative of real scenes (closed tank, sloped wall, V60 cone, partially filled bed) — a self-consistent D/G pair must not be able to pass while the seeded solve injects pressure through a different operator, and boundary-adjacent restriction/prolongation is exactly where multigrid-like schemes break, so box-only tests are insufficient; a two-grid convergence factor measured on known low- and high-frequency modes of those masked domains; then post-projection RMS divergence — measured with the projection's own divergence operator, interior cells only (surface cells are gated in U4) — drops by at least a stated factor (≥2×) per doubling of the fine-sweep budget until it falls below a pre-registered absolute tolerance, where the tolerance is derived from the visible-error budget (the xpbd density-band envelope) and the sweep count needed to reach it must fit the rung's pre-registered wall-clock ceiling (meeting tolerance only at non-real-time sweep counts fails L0 now, not L3 later); because the self-measured divergence gate is gameable, independent volume gates run alongside it: particle-density drift band, tank water-height drift, zero net flux through closed walls, free-surface fill-fraction conservation; a floor that plateaus above tolerance FAILS the gate (monotonic-but-plateauing was exactly main's A≠D·G signature); run on both a static tank and mid-splash dam break; A/B coarse-seed-on vs off shows the seed removes low-frequency residual (tank pressure profile approaches hydrostatic); with the adaptive-Tait predictor enabled, an A/B run must show a measurable fine-iteration-budget reduction at equal divergence tolerance — below threshold the predictor is removed rather than shipped dormant; density band on settled dam break matches the existing xpbd water-gate envelope; cost gate — dispatches/frame within the recorded U1 budget + a stated increment; long-run (≥2000 steps) settled tank stays settled (no pressure-driven creep or popcorn).
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:160:- **Goal:** A fast jet into a pool digs a visible, transiently sustained cavity — the L0 exit demonstration that this architecture fixes the PBF failure.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:165:- **Test scenarios:** jet at pour velocity into a settled pool produces a cavity whose depth exceeds a stated multiple of jet radius and persists ≥ a stated frame count — floors pre-committed BEFORE implementation begins (depth ≥ 1 jet radius; persistence ≥ the gravity re-leveling timescale at pour velocity; both against the documented xpbd baseline of ≈0), with empirical calibration allowed to tighten thresholds above the floors but never loosen below (a gate calibrated to its own output cannot fail, which would make the no-fallback bet's first stopping rule vacuous); cavity collapses and the surface re-levels after the jet stops (no permanent hole); surface particles don't gain energy (no popcorn) over a long run; volume conservation through cavity formation/collapse; the U3 assembled-operator, divergence-decay, and independent volume gates RE-RUN after the surface Dirichlet and bubble rows are inserted — the L0 exit operator is the one with surface handling in it, and U3's certification does not transfer to a materially different operator.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:193:- **Test scenarios:** drained flux through a static saturated column scales ∝ K(φ)·head and ∝ d² across a grind sweep (ordering + ratio bands); water entering the bed surface sees no pressure-jump barrier — entry flux matches the head-driven Darcy prediction within a stated band (the anti-divergence-penalty gate); drag pair update conserves combined momentum per node exactly; a settled saturated column stays settled long-run (drag does not pump energy); a submerged dense block in hydrostatic water exerts/receives the correct buoyant reaction — measured as the reaction impulse on the CONSTRAINED (frozen) solid equal to displaced-volume weight, since a frozen body has no dynamics to observe (dynamic buoyant motion is gated in U7); an undrained vertical load on a sealed saturated column transfers fully to pore pressure via the boundary reaction (the quantitative Skempton-B gate lives in U7 — a kinematically frozen skeleton has no volumetric strain path, so B is not meaningful here); the drag↔projection split is gated as a coupled system rather than assumed stable from the fold alone: under timestep refinement, slip-velocity decay matches the analytic exponential within a band with no oscillation growth and no energy increase (the partitioned-coupling gate at ρ_f/ρ_s ≈ 0.83); β-variation stress tests — high slip (nonlinear regime), dry→wet transition, φ near packing — show bounded error against a subcycled reference, where the reference subcycles drag AND projection together on a closed variable-φ two-phase column with outflow (the hard case is the full coupled loop, not drag decay in isolation); face-velocity consistency check — the velocities feeding D(φv) carry the same drag, pressure, solids-pressure, and boundary contributions as the momentum update (the multiphase Rhie-Chow corollary from KTD-2; a manufactured field where omitting any one term produces a detectable spurious φ_s oscillation); the U3 divergence-decay gate re-runs on a variable-φ saturated column against the mixture divergence (operator consistency in the φ<1 regime — the regime the novelty claim lives in); free-water region (φ_f = 1) behavior is byte-identical to U3 results (coupling terms vanish where there is no bed).
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:205:- **Test scenarios:** infiltration into a dry wettable bed starts on contact (no interpenetration delay) and early-time rate exceeds the bare-Darcy rate when suction is enabled (Green–Ampt regime A/B); with the bloom gate enabled, a dry bed shows a seconds-scale entry delay then accelerates (two-regime discrimination); pour at demand > capacity ponds, pond depth stabilizes, and after pour stop the pond drains within the calibrated band (medium-grind scenario mapped to reduced units); total volume (free + absorbed + pore) conserved exactly over the run including a ≥2000-step saturated tail; absorption conserves momentum under the effective-mass convention; a manufactured source-term test pins the s_net plumbing — prescribed local s_f/s_s in a closed saturated bed must produce the compensating pore-water flux and pressure response through the projection RHS with no density drift (total-volume conservation alone cannot catch a projection that ignores the sources); grains swell to the calibrated magnitude and the local K drops accordingly (monotonic gate); water drains through the filter boundary while grains are retained (phase-selective boundary gate).
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:216:- **Approach:** Release the solid field under saturated conditions: pore pressure feeds effective stress (Bishop saturation weighting) into the return map; wet cohesion from the existing curve; impact momentum arrives through the shared-grid drag/contact path (no pairwise impulse bolt-ons — the named failed approach). Crater metric = center surface depression vs rim, against the swelling baseline.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:217:- **Test scenarios:** center pour produces a crater whose depth exceeds the swelling lift (signed metric > 0, vs the documented main/xpbd baselines of ≈0); crater walls slump when the jet stops (tensile-apex behavior, no frozen spikes); saturated bed under sustained pour does not fluidize at calibrated drag (skeleton holds, per the xpbd gate); the Terzaghi 1D consolidation benchmark gates the pore-pressure/effective-stress coupling — sudden load on a saturated column, pore-pressure dissipation U(T) matches the analytic series solution within a stated band, run at TWO drainage lengths so the T = c_v·t/H² collapse verifies the scaling rather than one tuned curve, with the load-sharing check that effective stress + pore pressure sum to the applied total load throughout, and the quantitative Skempton-B gate (undrained pore-pressure response ratio ≈ 1 for incompressible constituents) measured here on the DEFORMABLE skeleton — U6's frozen skeleton has no volumetric strain path, so this is the first unit where B is meaningful (the standard geotech u-p validation; note the Vermeer-Verruijt caveat that too-small drainage steps produce spurious pore-pressure oscillation); stress-partition audit — in a static saturated column, total vertical stress equals effective stress + pore pressure within tolerance (no volumetric mode resisted twice across contact, cap, solids-pressure guard, and projection pressure, per KTD-5); per-frame impulse ledger — pressure, drag, gravity, and boundary/filter reaction impulses account for each phase's momentum change separately, with open-boundary (filter outflow) fluxes tracked explicitly; combined long-run conservation (volume incl. swelling, momentum) on the full V60 pour scene; the L2 ponding/drain bands re-run on the deformable bed (swelling and plastic compaction shift the K field the bands were gated under); a reduced divergence-decay check re-runs with the deformable bed; determinism — two seeded runs produce identical metrics.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:225:- **Files:** modify `src/solvers/twofield/mod.rs` (Metrics population, diagnostics surface `read_*`/`sample_diagnostics`), `docs/PERF_NOTES.md`, `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` (solver section); create `examples/twofield_scaling.rs` (mirror `examples/scaling_probe.rs`); test `tests/twofield_perf.rs` (budget + no-sync smoke — keeps `tests/twofield_scaffold.rs` owned by U1).
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:226:- **Approach:** Scaling probe across 5k→200k on water-only and full scenes; per-pass timestamp breakdown via `profile()`; compare against the xpbd baseline (profiler-as-referee, recorded in PERF_NOTES); populate `Metrics` (drawdown time, evenness placeholders where extraction is deferred); ensure the test readback surface matches the xpbd precedent so future suites can port.
