docs/plans/2026-06-18-001-feat-twofield-surface-weighted-dissipation-plan.md:168:- **In-repo reference (mechanism):** XPBD XSPH viscosity with surface falloff — `src/solvers/xpbd/water.wgsl:226-267`; the surface-weakening rationale in `src/web.rs` (WaterOnly `xsph_viscosity_c` comment). main's ballistic-preserve G2P — `crates/sim-wasm/src/mpm_3d/shader.rs` (reference only).
docs/plans/2026-06-18-001-feat-twofield-surface-weighted-dissipation-plan.md:169:- **Prior art:** ASFLIP — Fei, Guo, Wu, Huang, Gao, *Revisiting Integration in the Material Point Method*, SIGGRAPH 2021 (APIC + adaptive-α FLIP for separation/less dissipation). PolyPIC — Fu et al., SIGGRAPH Asia 2017 (higher moments; heavier — not adopted). FLIP energy-gain + mitigations — Boyd & Bridson, *MultiFLIP*, TOG 2012; APIC — Jiang et al., SIGGRAPH 2015. Adaptive compression–vorticity blend — Yao & Zhao, arXiv 2603.03860, 2026 (signal refinement).
docs/plans/2026-06-18-001-feat-twofield-surface-weighted-dissipation-plan.md:170:- **Constraints / learnings:** AGENTS.md (16-buffer cap, no float atomics, Tint uniformity, operator consistency A=D·M̃⁻¹·G, don't-touch-xpbd); `docs/PERF_NOTES.md` (R9 at 25.4 ms@207k, ~7.6 ms headroom, bubble_fine/jacobi_fine dominate); the crater-hold + settled-quiet PIC-blend contract (`docs/plans/2026-06-15-001-...`); volume-conservation hard constraint + saturated-tail leak + reorder stale-phase artifact (MEMORY).
src/utils/kernels.rs:4://! Poly6 for density, spiky gradient for the pressure/correction forces — the standard PBF
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:19:The fix is the textbook **unresolved (semi-resolved) SPH-DEM coupling** (Robinson et al. 2013; Macklin "Unified Particle Physics" 2014): keep PBF for water and rigid XPBD contact for sand-sand, and couple the two phases through (a) the **volumetric solid-fraction field** `α_s` the solver already computes (water density targets `ρ₀·(1−α_s)`, so water packs into pore volume) **backed by an explicit retention floor** so it actually resists free-fall, and (b) a **physically-grounded Darcy drag as a symmetric capped-pair impulse, Jacobi-subiterated** (implicit/non-overshooting via the bounded blend) strong enough to both set drainage *and* mobilize the bed surface. Only **after** that drag is landed and characterized is the old exclusion removed. Water particles are made genuinely finer than grains, with a **wider coupling kernel** so the porosity/drag field stays smooth.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:29:- **Architecture (user-directed, confirmed by literature):** geometric asymmetry. Sand↔sand: rigid XPBD contact at full sand radius. Water↔water: PBF density at the small water radius. Water↔sand: **no sphere collision** — porosity field (with a retention floor) + relative-velocity drag.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:32:  - **8 storage buffers per shader stage** is a hard WebGPU ceiling (`drag_water`/`drag_grain`/`buoyancy_grain`/`compute_lambda`/`compute_dp`/`bed_project` are already at 8/8). Browser/Tint portable.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:42:- **R3.** Couple the porosity field and drag through a **coupling kernel wider than the water PBF kernel** (≈ 2–3× grain diameter) for a smooth void fraction; the **water PBF keeps its own query radius** even though the grid cell grows; clamp porosity `ε ≥ ~0.35`. Instrument avg/max neighbor counts.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:45:- **R6.** **Buoyancy stays a separate pressure-gradient term**, not folded into drag, and is **not double-counted** with the α_s-modified pressure field; the density-aware "don't launch dense grains" fix remains intact.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:47:- **R8.** Behavior is verified by **banded** causal-lever inequalities: coarse-grind drains faster than fine (ratio band); pour-zone grain displacement within a band; bulk displacement under a ceiling; water retention height within a band; finer resolution does not change per-grain drag (invariance) or erupt.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:54:- **KTD-2 — Retention = density-target porosity floor (static) + strong drag (dynamic); no separate body force; remove exclusion only after drag lands.** `α_s` density modulation alone is **not** a hard barrier (a sparse/under-neighbored water particle can advect through, pool below, or ghost in high-α_s zones — Codex r1.1). But a separate `∇α_s` body force is the wrong fix (Codex r2.3/r2.6): it adds another pressure/volume-force channel that fights the buoyancy decoupling and needs its own conservation exception, and a "near-saturation drag limit" can't retain at zero velocity. The reframe: **static retention is moot — at zero relative velocity water is not advecting, so there is nothing to retain.** A resting saturated column is held by PBF **incompressibility** + a **minimum-porosity floor** in the water density target (`target = ρ₀·(1−min(α_s, 1−ε_floor))`, pure PBF, static, momentum-conserving) so water cannot over-pack into grain space; a *moving* wetting front is slowed to the **Darcy velocity by the strong drag** (KTD-3) — percolation, not free-fall. So retention needs **no new force**: density floor + drag. Sequencing: **land the drag first (U3), remove exclusion under it (U2)**, gated by a "no free-fall through a static bed" + bounded-flux test (Codex r1.2/r1.12). If the floor+drag combination empirically still leaks, a *conservative* capillary term is a deferred contingency — not in this plan.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:56:- **KTD-4 — Mobilization is a separate, *measurable* criterion from stability (Codex r1.5/r2.4).** Viscous drag damps; bed *rearrangement* requires the pour-zone per-frame drag impulse on a grain to **exceed** its yield resistance, measured concretely from quantities the solver already tracks: the XPBD friction budget `friction_mu · normal_impulse[i]` (accumulated contact load) **plus** the grain's submerged weight `(ρ_grain_eff − ρ_w)·V·g`. The gate is a **probe test**: instrument, for pour-zone grains, `|drag_impulse| > friction_mu·normal_impulse + submerged_weight·dt` (yields/moves), and for bulk grains the reverse (stays below fluidization). This makes the inequality measurable, not post-hoc tuning.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:57:- **KTD-5 — Wide coupling kernel `h_c`, water PBF keeps its own radius.** Compute `α_s`/drag porosity over `h_c ≈ 2–3× grain_diameter` for a smooth field; set `cell_size = max(h_c, grain_diameter, h)`, but the **water PBF density solve still distance-culls to its own `h`** (Codex r1.6) — the larger cell only widens the candidate list. Clamp `ε ∈ [0.35, 0.999]`. Instrument avg/max neighbor counts per pass (the wider gather can spike PBF/drag/sort cost).
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:58:- **KTD-6 — Reduced units; calibrate by BANDED multi-observable inequalities (Codex r1.8).** No SI system is wired (`KEEP.md §2`). Calibrate the one free drag scale against *several* bands simultaneously: coarse/fine drawdown ratio, bounded pour-zone displacement, a bulk-displacement ceiling, water-retention height, and zero long-run momentum/volume drift — one observable is under-determined. Real-unit conversion is deferred.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:59:- **KTD-7 — Buoyancy separate AND not double-counted with drag (Codex r1.10/r2.6).** Buoyancy stays `−V·∇P` over the PBF `λ` field (density-aware `grain_buoyancy_factor`); drag forces grains only from **relative velocity**. Because KTD-2 drops the `∇α_s` body force, there is **no third pressure channel** to conflict — the only pressure force on grains is buoyancy, the only velocity force is drag, cleanly separated. Gate: a hydrostatic saturated bed at rest has zero net grain acceleration beyond buoyancy balance; a flowing bed receives drag from relative velocity only.
docs/plans/2026-06-06-006-feat-water-grain-coupling-physics-plan.md:62:- **KTD-10 — Water finer than grains is realism, sequenced last with drag already resolution-normalized.** `R_w < R_s` improves the look + fluid detail but stresses CFL/kernel normalization (XSPH eruption lesson). Because KTD-3 resolution-normalizes drag, refinement won't change bed forcing; still run the spacing-invariance gate. Keep the count modest; defer the 200k push.
